# SemText npl-example — the two rendered states of the NPL pattern demo
# Direction A "Slate Console". One reference-page frame, two panels: the
# enhanced state and the JS-off state, so the tag's whole render contract is
# readable at a glance. npl-example is the reference implementation every
# other npl-* component is copied from, so the drawing must make the PATTERN
# legible: a generated label leads, authored content follows, and nothing is
# ever hidden.
#
# MODE 1 — enhanced (Lit component active, the authored example
#           `<npl-example label="hello">authored children survive</npl-example>`):
#   visible: a small block-level generated LABEL element sitting on its own
#     line at the TOP of the block (a squared violet-accented chip — the
#     generated chrome), and the authored body content beneath it, fully
#     readable, untouched. The label and the body are on separate lines.
#   must never: hide, dim, or occlude the authored body; fuse the label
#     inline with the body text on one run (the label is block-level and
#     leads); repeat the label; render the label as a heading or title.
#
# MODE 2 — JS-off (no script: the same authored markup, nothing generated):
#   visible: the SAME block, but with NO label chip at all — only the
#     authored body content, reading as plain prose in a block box. Nothing
#     is hidden and nothing is added.
#   must never: show a label chip or any generated chrome; hide or reorder
#     the authored content; insert placeholder chrome.
#
# The two panels must be pixel-comparable in body placement: the ONLY
# difference is the presence (enhanced) or absence (JS-off) of the label
# chip above the body.
#
# Reference snapshots come from the snapshot-harness lane
# (`npm run snap:tag -- npl-example`, output into
# .tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-example/). The harness was not runnable
# when this prompt was written (its driver fails to parse — see
# reports/tag-npl-example.md), so the attachments block below is left
# commented out; attach the best `-render-` PNGs there once it is fixed.
schema: '0.4'
id: reference-npl-example
type: image
quality: high
service: gemini
model: gemini-2.5-flash-image

prompt:
  text: |
    Dark-mode UI mockup of two side-by-side reference-page panels illustrating
    one display-only element in its two rendered states, wide 16:9, flat
    technical illustration (screenshot-like, not 3D). Dark slate navy
    background (#0F172A) with a barely visible thin grid and a faint violet
    radial glow upper-left. Two equal panels sit side by side, separated by a
    thin slate hairline divider, each panel a dark slate card (#1E293B) with a
    thin slate hairline border, squared corners, deep soft shadow; a small dim
    caption bar sits above each panel.

    Left panel — ENHANCED (the script/custom-element active). Inside, aligned
    to the panel's left content column: at the TOP a single small block-level
    LABEL chip — a compact squared rectangle with a thin violet (#7C3AED)
    hairline border and a soft-violet (#A78BFA) fill at low opacity, sized for
    one short word, containing two or three short muted placeholder bars that
    stand for the label text (no real letters). Directly BENEATH the chip,
    separated by a clear vertical gap so it clearly sits on its own line, the
    authored BODY: three or four muted slate placeholder bars of varying
    length forming a short paragraph, at full opacity and clearly legible as
    the dominant content. The chip is visibly smaller and secondary to the
    body; the body is never tinted, dimmed, or crossed by any overlay.

    Right panel — JS-OFF (no script). The same panel layout and the same
    authored BODY bars in exactly the same position and length as the left
    panel, at the same full opacity — but with NO label chip above it and no
    generated chrome of any kind: the top of the panel's content column is
    empty space where the chip would be, and the body simply reads as plain
    prose in the block. Nothing is hidden, nothing is added.

    The two panels must be pixel-comparable: the body bars align to the same
    baseline and length, and the ONLY difference between the panels is the
    presence (left) or absence (right) of the label chip. All copy is abstract
    placeholder bars — strictly no letters, digits, or words anywhere.
  negative: legible text, letters, numbers, digits, words, captions,
    typography, watermark, logo, light mode, white cards, rounded corners,
    3D, photorealism, faces, avatars, robot mascots, more than two panels,
    a label chip inline with the body on one run, a label chip rendered as a
    heading or oversized title, a repeated label, a hidden or dimmed body,
    any overlay or blur crossing the body text
  style: flat dark-mode product UI illustration, squared corners, hairline
    borders, slate and violet palette, two-panel state comparison

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
  dimensions:
    width: 1600
    height: 900
    aspect_ratio: '16:9'

# attachments:
#   - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-example/npl-example-render-1280.png
#     role: reference
#     description: enhanced state — generated label chip leads, authored body beneath
#   - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-example/npl-example-source-1280.png
#     role: reference
#     description: the authored markup and its reference-page chrome

requirements:
  subject: two panels of one display-only element — left enhanced with a small
    generated label chip above the authored body, right JS-off with the same
    body and no generated chrome
  palette: slate navy background, slate panels, hairline borders, one violet
    label chip (thin violet border, soft-violet low-opacity fill)
  composition: two equal panels side by side, body content aligned to the same
    baseline and length in both, label chip present only in the left panel and
    leading the body on its own line
  do:
    - place the label chip on its own line above the body in the enhanced panel
    - keep the authored body identical and fully legible in both panels
    - remove all generated chrome in the JS-off panel
  dont:
    - any legible text, digits, or logos
    - the label chip fused inline with the body text
    - a hidden, dimmed, or occluded body
    - a label chip in the JS-off panel

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.7
  required_pass:
    - render_states
    - body_intact
  criteria:
    render_states:
      weight: 3
      description: Left panel shows the enhanced state (a generated label chip
        leading, on its own line, above the body); right panel shows the JS-off
        state (the same body, no generated chrome at all)
      fail_signals:
        - a label chip present in the JS-off panel
        - no label chip in the enhanced panel
        - the two panels otherwise identical such that the states are
          indistinguishable
    body_intact:
      weight: 3
      description: The authored body is fully visible, legible and identically
        placed in both panels — never hidden, dimmed, or occluded
      fail_signals:
        - body dimmed, blurred, crossed by an overlay, or hidden
        - body text changed in length or position between panels
    label_not_fused:
      weight: 2
      description: In the enhanced panel the label chip is block-level — it sits
        on its own line and leads the body, never run inline with the body text
      fail_signals:
        - label chip rendered inline on the same line as the body
        - label chip rendered as a heading or oversized title
    theme_fidelity:
      weight: 1
      description: Dark slate + violet, squared hairline panels, flat style
      fail_signals:
        - light theme
        - glossy 3D look
        - rounded corners
  reject_if:
    - any legible text, letters, digits, words, watermark, or logo
    - a hidden, dimmed, or occluded authored body
    - more than two panels or a chaotic layout

tags:
  - semtext
  - reference
  - npl-example